Common mistakes include:
using a method whose assumptions are not met, such as a t test on badly skewed data with a small sample;
confusing the standard deviation of the data with the standard error of an estimate;
reading a p value as the probability that the null hypothesis is true;
saying a result is important when it is only statistically significant;
treating a correlation as proof of cause;
dropping outliers without saying so;
reporting too many decimal places, or none at all;
and describing a sample as if it were the population.

Many of these come from rushing to a formula before asking what the data are and how they were collected. Read the study design first, then choose the method.
